\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces Gains of the $30$ published pairs ($28$ with a sparse baseline, $2$ with a dense one; Table~\ref {tab:pairs}) under retrieval or retrieval-proxy metrics, in percentage points, with the KG-derived census component $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle \mathrm  {C}$}\mathaccent "0362{\mathrm  {C}}_{\mathcal  {K}}$. Each row summarizes the same-paper published pairs for one benchmark: the number of pairs and the median, minimum, and maximum reported gain. STaRK appears as its three released sub-benchmarks, Amazon, MAG, and Prime, which share the relation-traversal construction that gives $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle \mathrm  {C}$}\mathaccent "0362{\mathrm  {C}}_{\mathcal  {K}}=0.75$. The GraphRAG-Bench pairs use a dense published baseline and are excluded from the median trend; all other rows use sparse baselines, and negative cases are retained. Benchmark medians increase with $\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle \mathrm  {C}$}\mathaccent "0362{\mathrm  {C}}_{\mathcal  {K}}$, with descriptive Spearman $\rho =0.86$ over the eleven sparse-baseline medians. This association is reported descriptively for the reasons given in the text. The largest median and single gain are in \textbf  {bold}.}}{28}{table.caption.52}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:scatter}{{18}{28}{Gains of the $30$ published pairs ($28$ with a sparse baseline, $2$ with a dense one; Table~\ref {tab:pairs}) under retrieval or retrieval-proxy metrics, in percentage points, with the KG-derived census component $\Chat _{\kg }$. Each row summarizes the same-paper published pairs for one benchmark: the number of pairs and the median, minimum, and maximum reported gain. STaRK appears as its three released sub-benchmarks, Amazon, MAG, and Prime, which share the relation-traversal construction that gives $\Chat _{\kg }=0.75$. The GraphRAG-Bench pairs use a dense published baseline and are excluded from the median trend; all other rows use sparse baselines, and negative cases are retained. Benchmark medians increase with $\Chat _{\kg }$, with descriptive Spearman $\rho =0.86$ over the eleven sparse-baseline medians. This association is reported descriptively for the reasons given in the text. The largest median and single gain are in \best {bold}}{table.caption.52}{}}
